#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158579 :tutorial/content "Two statements are **logically equivalent** if they have identical truth values in their respective truth tables. In this lesson, we'll use truth tables to show that two complex compound statements are logically equivalent.\n\nIf two statements $P$ and $Q$ are logically equivalent, we write $$P\\equiv Q$$ where the symbol \"$\\equiv$\" means \"is logically equivalent to.\"\n\nFor example, let's use a truth table to prove the following logical equivalence:\n\n$$(A \\land \\neg A) \\lor B \\equiv B$$\n\nFirst, we construct truth tables with columns $A$ and $B$ containing all possible combinations of truth values. Then, we create another column for $\\neg A,$ which has the opposite truth values to $A.$\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\, \\neg A \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n| $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n| $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n\nNow, we add a column for $A \\land \\neg A.$ Recall that $A\\land \\neg A$ is true if $A$ is true and $\\neg A$ is true and is false otherwise. In this case, $A\\land \\neg A$ is always false.\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\, \\neg A \\,$ | $\\, A \\land \\neg A \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n| $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n| $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n| $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n\nFinally, we complete the table with a column for $(A \\land \\neg A) \\lor B.$ Recall that $(A \\land \\neg A) \\lor B$ is true if $(A \\land \\neg A)$ is true, or $B$ is true, or both are true.\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\, \\neg A \\,$ | $\\, A \\land \\neg A \\,$ | $\\, (A \\land \\neg A) \\lor B \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n\nWe see that the truth values of $(A \\land \\neg A) \\lor B$ are the same as the truth values of $B.$ This proves that $(A \\land \\neg A) \\lor B$ is logically equivalent to $B.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "52050e7d-e6dd-5b8e-a1f9-56a8c00bbaa7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8260 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
